  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe propose a multi-resolution optimization algorithm that combines iterative projection techniques with adaptive grid refinement. Unlike previous approaches that directly optimize on fixed grids, our method leverages the solution anatomy (underlying structure) of step function approximations to:\n   - Identify repeating patterns in optimal solutions\n   - Construct multi-scale candidate bases\n   - Use cross-scale transfer learning to improve accuracy\nThe key insight is that near-optimal solutions often exhibit sparse, periodic structures that can be truncated to lower resolutions. The central idea is to iteratively refine these structures through (a) spectral projection, (b) cross-frequency information transfer, and (c) basis vector selection from a learned dictionary.\n\nThis approach addresses the convergence bottlenecks of local optimizations by introducing:\n   - Analytic divergence estimates to direct search\n   - Optimization-invariant structure validation\n   - Goal-oriented adaptive grid generation (n=300\u2192150, 500, 1000)\nThe method can escape local optima by projecting gradients through the solution anatomy, effectively providing \"intrinsic coordinates\" for the optimization space.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe represent h(x) as a finite sum:\n   h(x) = \u03a3\u1d62 \u03b8\u1d62 \u03c6\u1d62(x)\nwhere \u03c6\u1d62 are wavelets or Gabor functions as base functions. The anatomy consists of:\n   Constraint space: {h | \u222bh dx=1, h\u2208[0,1]}\n   Objective: min( max\u209a ||\u27e8h, (1-translation-h)(cross spectrum)||\u2081 )\nThe optimization uses:\n   - Projected gradient descent with Nesterov momentum\n   - Sparse convex combinations (matrix factorization)\n   - Discrete cosine transform for basis selection\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   Step 1: Initialize anatomy dictionary (\u0393) with 300 points using:\n     - Provided parent solution (if available)\n     - Slepian sequences for solution skeletons\n\n   Step 2: Define triplet-based anatomy transfer:\n        Training set (X): 1000 solution candidates\n        Structural features: Mellin transforms, R\u00e9nyi entropy, and frequency powers\n\n   Step 3: Core loop:\n        For \u03c4 in range(acceleration_factor):\n            K = ceil(1.5*\u03c4)\n            n_candidates = min(1000, budget*K)\n            (h, c5) = pareto_solver(h_proposed, resolution=n, time=deadline_time)\n            anatomy_update(h, c5, anatomy_dict)\n\n   Step 4: Joining and projection:\n        h_projected = Tdict * h_dict (dictionary learning)\n        h_projected = project_feasible(h_projected, n)\n\n   Step 5: Anomaly detection and resolution switching:\n        if (c5_improvement < 1e-6) over 10 iterations:\n            seek_alternative_resolution(n)\n            deadline = max(deadline - 400, 10)\n        else if (c5 below target) break;\n\nImplementation blocks:\n\n    def project_feasible(h, n):\n        target = n/2\n        h_clipped = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n        diff = target - np.sum(h_clipped)\n        while abs(diff) > 1e-12 and attempts < 1000:\n            # Perform distribution adjustment\n        return h\n\n    def seek_alternative_resolution(n):\n        morphology_features = get_morphology_features(initial_h_values)\n        optimal_n = predict_optimal_resolution(morphology_features)\n        h_new = resample(h, optimal_n)\n        return h_new\n\n4. Search Choices\n   - Initialize n_candidates: Exponential sweep from 100 to 1500 points\n   - Dictionary size: Auto-tuned by coherence factor (KS < 0.15)\n   - Projection factor: 1.0 \u2192 3.0 based on relative objective improvement\n   - Basis selection: LMA-DFRFT (Locally-Minimized Ambiguity Distribution Fourier Transform)\n\n5. Compute Budget\n   Baseline computation: O(n\u00b2 log n) per resolution (n=1500: 1500*1500*log(1500) \u2248 3.375e9 operations -> ~5 minutes on modern hardware)\n   Time est: 200 seconds: n\u2264300, 400 seconds: n\u2264500, deadline: n\u22641000\n   Safety margin: 33% validation overhead\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   Phase 1: Re-evaluate candidate on orthogonal transform domain\n   Phase 2: Box-constraint verification: \u2200i h[i]\u2208[0,1], \u2211h[i]=n/2\n   Phase 3: authenticated_compare(c5_old, c5_new) using:\n        diff = abs(c5_old - c5_new) / 0.3808 + 1e-8\n        return diff < 1e-4\n\n   Returns format must be:\n        (h, c5_bound, n_points)\n   with c5_bound computed via:\n        c5_bound = compute_c5_vec(h, n) * dx\n\n   After optimization:\n        assert c5_bound < 0.38086, \"Target budget not met\"\n        assert n_points >= 100 and n_points <= 2000, \"Invalid resolution\"",
